  "detail": "Thank you: requirements (1) and (3) of the last round hold, and two kept jobs stay independent. The remaining gap is in requirement (2) as we wrote it last round. We asked for confirmation by \"a successful scan for that job's marker that finds no live processes\", and that was too permissive on our side: when the worker stopped after starting a process but before saving that invocation's discovery record, recovery scans with the fallback context from client.ts:290 (group 0, leader null), and an empty result there proves nothing. Reproduced against fdba89f0 through runWorker on macOS: a marked /bin/sh changes directory outside the job's roots and execs /bin/sleep, its launcher exits, the kept entry has no post-spawn discovery record, and after a restart the sleeper is still running while the job's root and scratch were removed. The same state follows a failed discovery save and any later worker death, not only a kill in the microseconds between spawn and save.\n\nRequired (this is the only new mechanism; keep everything else as round 2 made it):\n1. Record each invocation as pending before it spawns. Pass the invocation marker (already created in startProcess before `beforeSpawn` runs) to `beforeSpawn`, and save it in the kept entry's pending set in the same save that `beforeSpawn` already does. Use a set, not a flag, so concurrent invocations within one job are covered.\n2. A pending marker is cleared when that invocation's discovery record is saved, or when the running worker itself confirms that invocation's termination (the point where its cleanup records are deleted today, with the live child handle).\n3. At recovery (startup and later attempts), a kept entry with any pending marker that has no saved discovery record is confirmed only by a host boot identity different from the recorded one. A successful or empty scan does not confirm it. Keep its directories, log it as unresolved, and keep running other jobs, exactly as for other unresolved entries. If the entry has no recorded boot identity, record the current one at the first recovery attempt that can read it, so a later reboot can confirm it.\n4. Do not key this on the leader being null: on Linux the leader is always null, and recovery with saved discovery records must keep working there as it does now.\n5. The `confirmed` field is only ever assigned `false` and never read; the pending set may replace it.\n\nTests (they must fail on fdba89f0, and the first two must run on Linux):\n- A kept entry with a pending marker and no discovery record, an injected discover that returns [], and a restart: the directories and the entry are kept and the unresolved state is logged. Then a restart with a different boot identity removes them.\n- An invocation whose discovery record is saved normally leaves no pending marker, and a normal job still removes everything at job end.\n- Optionally, a macOS-only test of the /bin/sh -> cd outside -> exec /bin/sleep case above.\nIf an existing test such as \"worker retries a job with failed boot inspection on a later heartbeat\" conflicts with rule 3, keep rule 3 and say in your report which test changed and why. Run only the affected test files."
 }
